# Backup settings for backup.sh and restore.sh. Copy to backup.conf next
# to this file and edit. backup.conf is gitignored. It's read by bash, so
# no spaces around '=' and quote anything with spaces in it.

# Where the copy that leaves this box goes. Required. Either an
# rsync-over-SSH target or a mounted share. The directory has to exist,
# and an SSH target needs rsync installed on the far end and a key the
# backup user can log in with unattended (DEPLOY_DEBIAN.md Step 10).
# Backups copy there only if the folder holds a file named
# .mileage-backup-target, which install.sh writes at setup (or you write
# once with: bash backup.sh --mark-target). If a share isn't mounted when
# the timer runs, its empty folder has no such file, so the backup fails
# instead of quietly filling this machine's own disk.
REMOTE=backup@nas.lan:/volume1/backups/mileage-tracker
#REMOTE=/mnt/backup-share/mileage-tracker

# Extra ssh options for an SSH REMOTE, e.g. a dedicated key. backup.sh adds
# ServerAliveInterval=30, ServerAliveCountMax=4 and ConnectTimeout=30 unless
# you set ServerAliveInterval here, so a NAS that vanishes mid-copy ends the
# run in about two minutes instead of hanging it. (rsync itself gives up
# after 10 minutes without any data moving.)
#RSYNC_SSH="ssh -i /home/<user>/.ssh/mileage_backup"

# Local snapshots. Default: ~/mileage-backups of the user the timer runs as.
#BACKUP_DIR=/home/<user>/mileage-backups

# Retention for local snapshots: the newest KEEP_RECENT, plus the first
# snapshot of each of the newest KEEP_MONTHLY months. Unchanged trip files
# are hard links between snapshots; each kept snapshot also holds full
# copies of the databases, so allow space for those.
# 84 months covers the IRS's longest common retention period (7 years).
KEEP_RECENT=14
KEEP_MONTHLY=84

# yes = after each copy, apply the same retention rule (KEEP_RECENT newest,
# plus the first of each of the newest KEEP_MONTHLY months) to the snapshot
# names on the off-host copy, and delete the names it drops. It never makes
# the off-host copy mirror the local folder, so a rebuilt server with few
# local snapshots doesn't touch the history there. It also stays off, with a
# warning in the log, while the off-host copy has a snapshot newer than this
# server's newest, or snapshots the rule would keep but this server lacks
# (a rebuilt server or a changed BACKUP_DIR); it resumes once this server has
# them again (the warning shows the rsync command that copies them back), or
# set this to no. Default no: this script then never prunes old remote
# snapshots. Protection from a compromised server needs retention/snapshots
# enforced on the backup host or a restricted account; ordinary write access
# can still delete or overwrite history.
REMOTE_PRUNE=no

# Optional URL to GET after each successful backup, for a dead man's
# switch (Uptime Kuma push monitor, healthchecks.io, ...). If the pings
# stop arriving, backups have stopped working.
#PING_URL=https://hc-ping.com/your-uuid

# The directory holding docker-compose.yml. Default: the parent of this
# backup/ directory, i.e. ~/mileage-tracker.
#COMPOSE_DIR=/home/<user>/mileage-tracker
